## 4. Tool conventions

- Register a ribbon tool in the toolbar spec **before** creating its folder. Every tool also needs `docs/toolbar/tools/<id>.md`.
- Firm tools go in their own panels inside `extensions/{{ extension }}.extension/{{ tab }}.tab/`. Never add them to the generated sample panel.
- Set `__author__` and `bundle.yaml author:` to `{{ author }}`. Personal attribution belongs in commit history, not tool metadata.
- Custom WPF uses the generated resources in `specimens/wpf/Theme.xaml` and `Controls.xaml` (keys prefixed `{{ ns }}.`) instead of hard-coded colours or fonts.
- Point users to support at {{ support_url }}.

## 5. Revit safety

- Validate the document context (none, family, project) and the selection before showing UI or starting a transaction.
- Keep transactions narrow. Never wait for user input inside one. Commit or roll back explicitly in `try`/`except`.
- Linked documents are read-only: read from them, write results into the host document.
- New tools default to read-only. Write operations need `risk` and `requires_confirmation` set correctly in the toolbar spec.
- pyRevit runs IronPython 2.7 by default: no f-strings, annotations, or walrus operators in extension code.

## 6. Verification

- Run the validators in `validators/` and any tests in `tests/` before opening a PR.
- Static checks never prove Revit behaviour. Record live checks per tool and Revit version before claiming support.

## 7. Handoffs

For work spanning sessions, agents, or people, keep `docs/handoffs/<slug>-YYYY-MM-DD.md` with `## State`, `## Open` (next steps, blockers, rejected paths), and `## Incremental Edit Log`, and list it in `docs/handoffs/INDEX.md`.
